  <text xml:lang="ta" type="edition" corresp="#Ed">
    <body>
      <div type="mūlam" rend="parallel">
      <lg xml:id="TNV5">
      <l>iruṇilai<anchor xml:id="irunilai"/> yucci<anchor xml:id="ucci"/> mālai<anchor xml:id="malai"/> vaikaṟai<anchor xml:id="vaikarai"/></l>
<l>poruṇilai yanti<anchor xml:id="yanti"/> puṇarkkum poḻutē.<anchor xml:id="polute"/></l></lg>
<lg xml:lang="en">
  <l>The state of darkness, midday, evening, daybreak,</l> 
  <l>and twilight are the times [of day] connected<note place="foot" xml:lang="en"><q xml:lang="ta-Latn"><hi rend="italic">puṇarkkum</hi></q> is derived from the strong (transitive) root, so one expects an object. The <foreign xml:lang="ta-Latn">tiṇai</foreign>-s are understood as the elliptical object: these are the times of day that connect to the <foreign xml:lang="ta-Latn">tiṇai</foreign>-s. <q xml:lang="ta-Latn"><hi rend="italic">poruṇilai</hi></q> qualifies this connection (see following note). One could alternatively take <q xml:lang="ta-Latn"><hi rend="italic">puṇarkkum</hi></q> as combining the listed times with each other, in which case they would collectively constitute the <q xml:lang="ta-Latn"><hi rend="italic">poḻutu</hi></q>. But this does not account for the transitive root, and it leaves <q xml:lang="ta-Latn"><hi rend="italic">poruṇilai</hi></q> without a clear function.</note> in [their] established sense<note place="foot" xml:lang="en">This edition takes <q xml:lang="ta-Latn"><hi rend="italic">poruṇilai</hi></q> as part of the predicate rather than as a modifier of <q xml:lang="ta-Latn"><hi rend="italic">anti</hi></q>. The phrase qualifies <q xml:lang="ta-Latn"><hi rend="italic">puṇarkkum poḻutē</hi></q>: the times of day are connected to the <foreign xml:lang="ta-Latn">tiṇai</foreign>-s according to an established arrangement. In the commentary, this is expanded by <q xml:lang="ta-Latn"><hi rend="italic">ēṟpāṭu muṟaiyāṉē ain tiṇaikkum</hi></q> (‘for the five <foreign xml:lang="ta-Latn">tiṇai</foreign>-s according to the established order’). <term xml:lang="ta-Latn"><hi rend="italic">poruḷ</hi></term> here is closer to ‘substance, essential nature’ than to ‘object’, similarly to what <q xml:lang="ta-Latn"><hi rend="italic">poruḷ nilai</hi></q> means in the <term xml:lang="ta-Latn"><hi rend="italic">Tolkāppiyam</hi></term> (cf. TC 252.2n). The <term xml:lang="ta-Latn"><hi rend="italic">etukai</hi></term> with <q xml:lang="ta-Latn"><hi rend="italic">iruṇilai</hi></q> is probably incidental. The author applies <term xml:lang="ta-Latn"><hi rend="italic">etukai</hi></term> sporadically and only where easy and unobtrusive (see \S\ref{sec:cuttiram-form}). One could alternatively take <q xml:lang="ta-Latn"><hi rend="italic">poruṇilai</hi></q> as a compound modifying <q xml:lang="ta-Latn"><hi rend="italic">anti</hi></q>, giving ‘twilight in its essential state’, but this leaves <q xml:lang="ta-Latn"><hi rend="italic">poruṇilai</hi></q> without a correspondent in the commentary and makes the enumeration ambiguous. If <q xml:lang="ta-Latn"><hi rend="italic">poruḷ nilai anti</hi></q> is a single phrase, it is not clear whether five or six times of day are listed. Under the present reading, the enumeration is unambiguously fivefold.</note> [to the <foreign xml:lang="ta-Latn">tiṇai</foreign>-s].
</l></lg>
  </div>
</body>
</text>
 <text xml:lang="ta" type="edition" corresp="#Ed">
    <body>
      <head xml:lang="en">Commentary</head>
      <div type="commentary" rend="parallel">
      <p xml:id="TNV5comm1">eṉpatu ciṟu kālamutal ām āṟuṇarttutaṉutaliṟṟu.</p>
<p xml:lang="en">The quoted [<term xml:lang="ta-Latn"><hi rend="italic">cūttiram</hi></term>] intends to make known the way in which
the minor basic time-element occurs.</p></div>
<div type="commentary" rend="parallel">
<p xml:id="TNV5comm2">naṭuvu paṭṭa viruḷ yāmamuṅ katir kāyum uccip poḻutu mālaip poḻutum pular kālaiyum 
antiyum ēṟpāṭu<anchor xml:id="erpatu"/> muṟaiyāṉē yain tiṇaikkum ciṟu kāla mutal ām eṉṟa vāṟu. <num>5.</num></p>
<p xml:lang="en">The middle of the dark midnight, the midday time when the sun burns, the evening time, the time of dawn, and twilight are the basic small time-elements of the five poetic settings according to the established order.</p>
</div>     
</body>
      </text>
      <standOff type="notes1" corresp="#TNV5"><note xml:lang="ta" target="#malai"><term xml:lang="en">Cf. TP6n:</term> kāru mālaiyu mullai kuṟiñci | kūtir yāma meṉmaṉār pulavar ||</note>
<note xml:lang="ta" target="#vaikarai"><term xml:lang="en">Cf. TP8n:</term> vaikuṟu viṭiyaṉ maruta meṟpāṭu | neyta lātaṉ meypeṟat tōṉṟum ||</note>
<note xml:lang="ta" target="#ucci"><term xml:lang="en">Cf. TP9n:</term> naṭuvunilait tiṇaiyē naṇpakala vēṉiloṭu | muṭivunilai maruṅkiṉ muṉṉiya neṟittē ||</note></standOff>
<standOff type="notes4" corresp="#TNV5"><note xml:lang="en" target="#polute">See the note in the apparatus to TNV 4.</note>
<note xml:lang="en" target="#polute">It is also worth pointing out that the TNV only lists five times of day, whereas the TP gives six – both <term xml:lang="ta-Latn"><hi rend="italic">vaikarai</hi></term> (‘the hours before sunrise’) and <term xml:lang="ta-Latn"><hi rend="italic">viṭiyal</hi></term> (‘the hours after sunrise’) are appropriate in <term xml:lang="ta-Latn"><hi rend="italic">marutam</hi></term>.</note></standOff>
