<standOff type="notes4" corresp="#TNV5comm2"><note xml:lang="en" target="#erpatu"><p>This edition deviates 
  from the manuscript readings. C1 and C2<hi rend="subscript">ac</hi> read <q xml:lang="ta-Latn"><hi rend="italic">antiyumē muṟpāṭu</hi></q>, which would give ‘according to the earlier order’. 
  The combination of an <term xml:lang="ta-Latn"><hi rend="italic">-um</hi></term> particle 
  with a subsequent <term xml:lang="ta-Latn"><hi rend="italic">-ē</hi></term> particle is not attested elsewhere in this text or in the Caṅkam
  corpus, though one could object that the commentary is in a different register. 
  Were it not for this, <q xml:lang="ta-Latn"><hi rend="italic">muṟpāṭu muṟaiyāṉē</hi></q> would have been perfectly fine.
  UVC’s annotation to C2 suggests he may have decided against <q xml:lang="ta-Latn"><hi rend="italic">antiyumē</hi></q> for the same reason. 
  He emended in line with the IAC, where Nakkīrar assigns <term xml:lang="ta-Latn"><hi rend="italic">eṟpāṭu</hi></term> (‘sunset’) as the <term xml:lang="ta-Latn"><hi rend="italic">ciṟupoḻutu</hi></term> of <term xml:lang="ta-Latn"><hi rend="italic">neytal</hi></term>. 
  These readings would not have been orthographically differentiated in a palm-leaf MS.</p>
  <p>There is a formulaic argument in favour of UVC’s reading. In the second commentary paragraph to TNV 4 and TNV 7, the author uses the same template as here: a list of items joined by <term xml:lang="ta-Latn"><hi rend="italic">-um</hi></term>, with the final <term xml:lang="ta-Latn"><hi rend="italic">-um</hi></term> directly preceding <q xml:lang="ta-Latn"><hi rend="italic">muṟaiyāṉē ain tiṇaikkum</hi></q>. No word intervenes between the last enumerated item and <q xml:lang="ta-Latn"><hi rend="italic">muṟaiyāṉē</hi></q> in either of those passages. With sandhi dissolved, UVC’s reading produces <q xml:lang="ta-Latn"><hi rend="italic">antiyum eṟpāṭum muṟaiyāṉē</hi></q>, where the word-final <term xml:lang="ta-Latn"><hi rend="italic">-m</hi></term> of <q xml:lang="ta-Latn"><hi rend="italic">eṟpāṭum</hi></q> is assimilated to the initial <term xml:lang="ta-Latn"><hi rend="italic">m-</hi></term> of <q xml:lang="ta-Latn"><hi rend="italic">muṟaiyāṉē</hi></q>. This fits the TNV 4/TNV 7 template. The comma printed in Cām and Cām2 between <q xml:lang="ta-Latn"><hi rend="italic">antiyum</hi></q> and <q xml:lang="ta-Latn"><hi rend="italic">eṟpāṭu</hi></q> also suggests UVC understood <term xml:lang="ta-Latn"><hi rend="italic">eṟpāṭu</hi></term> as a distinct enumerated item. C1’s reading breaks this pattern with both the unusual <term xml:lang="ta-Latn"><hi rend="italic">-umē</hi></term> and the extra word <q xml:lang="ta-Latn"><hi rend="italic">muṟpāṭu</hi></q> between the enumeration and <q xml:lang="ta-Latn"><hi rend="italic">muṟaiyāṉē</hi></q>.</p>
  <p>The problem with UVC’s reading is that it produces a list of six times of day for five <term xml:lang="ta-Latn"><hi rend="italic">tiṇai</hi></term>-s, and that it cannot be reconciled with the <term xml:lang="ta-Latn"><hi rend="italic">cūttiram</hi></term>. The <term xml:lang="ta-Latn"><hi rend="italic">cūttiram</hi></term> enumerates five times of day, where <q xml:lang="ta-Latn"><hi rend="italic">poruḷ nilai</hi></q> modifies <q xml:lang="ta-Latn"><hi rend="italic">anti</hi></q> rather than naming a sixth. For UVC’s reading to work, <q xml:lang="ta-Latn"><hi rend="italic">poruḷ nilai</hi></q> would have to correspond to <term xml:lang="ta-Latn"><hi rend="italic">eṟpāṭu</hi></term> in the commentary. There is no attested meaning for <q xml:lang="ta-Latn"><hi rend="italic">poruḷ nilai</hi></q> that would refer to a time of day. If this is an auto-commentary, as I believe it is, the prose should gloss the verse neatly. A sixth item in the commentary that has no correspondent in the <term xml:lang="ta-Latn"><hi rend="italic">cūttiram</hi></term> is difficult to accept.</p>
  <p>The hypothetical list of six also does not map neatly onto the five <term xml:lang="ta-Latn"><hi rend="italic">tiṇai</hi></term>-s.
  If the TNV were following the TP, then <q xml:lang="ta-Latn"><hi rend="italic">pular kālai</hi></q> and <q xml:lang="ta-Latn"><hi rend="italic">anti</hi></q>
  ought to correspond to <term xml:lang="ta-Latn"><hi rend="italic">vaikarai</hi></term> and <term xml:lang="ta-Latn"><hi rend="italic">viṭiyal</hi></term>. 
  I have only found instances where <term xml:lang="ta-Latn"><hi rend="italic">anti</hi></term> is used to refer to an evening twilight, whereas 
  for this to work, it would have to refer to the period following dawn, as it would be a <term xml:lang="ta-Latn"><hi rend="italic">marutattiṟkku poḻutu</hi></term>. 
  The <term xml:lang="ta-Latn"><hi rend="italic">Tivākaram</hi></term> glosses it with quasi-synonyms that have the common meaning of ‘junction, meeting’. The one example that can refer to a time of day is <term xml:lang="ta-Latn"><hi rend="italic">canti</hi></term> (‘junction’, ‘evening, dusk’) in 1.112. There is a clear semantic link between a ‘junction’ and ‘dawn’, but without any attestations there is no reason to think this does not refer to dusk.</p>
  <p>The reading <q xml:lang="ta-Latn"><hi rend="italic">ēṟpāṭu</hi></q> in the sense of ‘established custom, system’ avoids the six-item problem and preserves the correspondence between <term xml:lang="ta-Latn"><hi rend="italic">cūttiram</hi></term> and commentary. As discussed in the note to the translation of the <term xml:lang="ta-Latn"><hi rend="italic">cūttiram</hi></term>, if one accepts some displacement of word order on account of <term xml:lang="ta-Latn"><hi rend="italic">etukai</hi></term>, <q xml:lang="ta-Latn"><hi rend="italic">ēṟpāṭu muṟaiyāṉē</hi></q> in the commentary glosses <q xml:lang="ta-Latn"><hi rend="italic">poruṇilai</hi></q> in the verse. It does break the TNV 4/TNV 7 enumerative template, but this is explained by the fact that <term xml:lang="ta-Latn"><hi rend="italic">ēṟpāṭu</hi></term> is not a sixth enumerated item but a gloss on an element in the <term xml:lang="ta-Latn"><hi rend="italic">cūttiram</hi></term> that falls outside the enumeration.</p></note></standOff>
